{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfSR7owxAkNGsZQQrKP9H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1206,"cache_read_input_tokens":13642,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1206},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b6adecb-ba7a-46bb-9eb3-830f7a6cad57","uuid":"b7a2ff33-afba-4537-a1b8-f510d1c63ccd","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:06:51.880Z","request_id":"req_011CfSR7oKFAx5jvbxuRUWmm"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CfSR7owxAkNGsZQQrKP9H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered ten new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical titles; base kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Same title as base.\"},{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected typo, planet, spacecraft, duration.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected name, date, decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected light factor.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"reason\":\"Corrected timezone, year, swapped units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered ten new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected counts, names, year, tilt.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected wind units and ocean depth.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected number separators.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected Challenger date.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3 ... about 4,5 years\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), Uranus encounter text, read\",\"reason\":\"Page states Voyager 2 and 4.5 years.\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"Ur anus ... Saturn: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states Neptune, Voyager 2, 5.5 hours.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's ... Jan. 31, 1987 ... approximately 2,5 hours\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives Nov. 4, 1985 and 2.5 hours.\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states 400 times.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives UT, 1986, 50,640 miles.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 discovered ten new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees\",\"corrects\":\"11 new moons ... Goethe ... 1687), three new rings ... eight rings ... 66 degrees\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives ten, Shakespeare, 1787, two/nine, 55.\"},{\"statement\":\"as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) ... boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives mph, ocean, 497 miles (800 km).\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Uranian moons are Ariel, Umbriel, Titania; Titan orbits Saturn.\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers)\",\"corrects\":\"17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page uses comma separators.\"},{\"statement\":\"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives Jan. 28, 1986.\"}]}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1206,"cache_read_input_tokens":13642,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1206},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b6adecb-ba7a-46bb-9eb3-830f7a6cad57","uuid":"38ed42c0-6db5-47c7-9da1-7e097e3d1601","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:07:07.826Z","request_id":"req_011CfSR7oKFAx5jvbxuRUWmm"}
{"duration_api_ms":47812,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0b6adecb-ba7a-46bb-9eb3-830f7a6cad57","total_cost_usd":0.0875218,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1206,"cache_read_input_tokens":27284,"output_tokens":5871,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2058},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1206,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5242,"cache_read_input_tokens":13642,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1206,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1206},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":5871,"cacheReadInputTokens":27284,"cacheCreationInputTokens":1206,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0689988,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":2058,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":16633,"outputTokens":378,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.018522999999999998,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":2,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered ten new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical titles; base kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Same title as base.\"},{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected typo, planet, spacecraft, duration.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected name, date, decimal.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected light factor.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"reason\":\"Corrected timezone, year, swapped units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered ten new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected counts, names, year, tilt.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected wind units and ocean depth.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected number separators.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected Challenger date.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4.5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3 ... about 4,5 years\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), Uranus encounter text, read\",\"reason\":\"Page states Voyager 2 and 4.5 years.\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"Ur anus ... Saturn: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states Neptune, Voyager 2, 5.5 hours.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2's short-range observations of the planet began Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2.5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's ... Jan. 31, 1987 ... approximately 2,5 hours\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives Nov. 4, 1985 and 2.5 hours.\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page states 400 times.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives UT, 1986, 50,640 miles.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 discovered ten new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 55 degrees\",\"corrects\":\"11 new moons ... Goethe ... 1687), three new rings ... eight rings ... 66 degrees\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives ten, Shakespeare, 1787, two/nine, 55.\"},{\"statement\":\"as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) ... boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives mph, ocean, 497 miles (800 km).\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ smaller moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Uranian moons are Ariel, Umbriel, Titania; Titan orbits Saturn.\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers)\",\"corrects\":\"17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page uses comma separators.\"},{\"statement\":\"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"citation\",\"source\":\"NASA Voyager 2 mission page (science.nasa.gov), read\",\"reason\":\"Page gives Jan. 28, 1986.\"}]}","ttft_ms":5408,"type":"result","duration_ms":48831,"uuid":"d3a1e363-1b7d-4cd9-8ba1-73ce8a2047d7","ttft_stream_ms":1408,"time_to_request_ms":105,"first_content_frame_ms":1409,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
